WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.390 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  myanmar is a country on the brink of  collapse  hundreds of thousands of people have  taken to the streets to protest against  a military coup they have been met with  brutal force  over 3 800 people have been arrested  many have been tortured and at least 780  have been killed in street battles the  military seized control on february 1st  after the election commission rejected  their unfounded claims of voter fraud in  the november general election  aung sang soo chi the de facto leader of  myanmar  is being detained under trumped-up  charges  following her resounding re-election in  november  what does this mean for the rest of asia  and what's being done  to stop the country sliding into further  chaos  our experts answer your questions  where is angsang tsuchi and what will  happen to her  she hasn't been seen since the coup  she's currently under house arrest  and i'm afraid she's likely to remain in  jail  very much very likely for the rest of  her life um  she's currently been charged with uh six  offenses ranging from the  ludicrously minor offense of illegally  importing  walkie-talkies to much more serious  offenses for instance of  accepting bribes and and breaking the  official secrets act  the army clearly trumped up some charges  because they want to get aung san tsuchi  out of the way  but it all hinges on whether the army's  really able to assert control  um and if it isn't you know if some kind  of compromise has to be brokered then  then  you know we could see aung san tsuchi in  power again  i think she could become a kind of  bargaining chip  in negotiations between the generals the  shadow government this is composed of  deposed mps from her own party  and the international community which is  trying to broker talks  but we first have to get to the  negotiating table and we're  very far away away from that now what  are myanmar's powerful neighbors  india and china doing about this china  has responded in a very  uh ambiguous fashion um when the coup  first happened it described it as a  major cabinet reshuffle  um china has extensive interests in  myanmar  it views myanmar as being an important  notch  on its belt and road it's massive  infrastructure project  um and it is very keen to see those  investments  be protected so it's it's i i think  basically  um china's stance is we don't really  mind who's in power  as long as our interests are being take  being taken care of  india's also sort of wary of of china's  interest in myanmar china seems to see  myanmar as a potential  outlet to the indian ocean and uh  and that's something that that i think  india is keen to curtail  but there's a lot going on in india at  the moment a a terrible second wave of  kirby 19  um lots of very hard fought uh state  elections  and a government that itself has some  sort of authoritarian tendencies uh  isn't  isn't that keen to to be sort of  stumping for democracy  around the world so india's response has  really been  quite muted why aren't western countries  and the un  intervening and what should they do  western countries are actually  intervening um they  have placed sanctions on the senior  general  um his his cronies within the military  um on their on their business interests  the un  isn't going to do anything except for  issues strongly word statements  and that is because china and russia sit  on the security council  and will have vetoed any efforts  to do something more robust this is  because um  china is loath to annoy whoever is in  charge in myanmar  and russia is in the same camp  actually interestingly russia's deputy  defense minister  showed up at the army's big bash it's  armed forces day  uh recently in in late march so russia  is very keen to keep  the army happy russia is another major  arms supplier  um to the generals and they don't want  to lose out on on the army's custom the  only thing sort of  beyond those kind of sanctions that many  burmese  actually have been calling for many many  of the student protesters have been  calling for  is to literally sort of send troops and  overturn the coup  but myanmar is just too far away and too  little known  and there's too little appetite for that  kind of conflict the idea that  america or any european country would  intervene by force um is is just  fanciful unfortunately so so um  uh i think the most that can be hoped  for is some kind of  smart sanction regime that sort of makes  life difficult  and um awkward for for the generals  how is the rohingya refugee crisis  affected by the situation  in myanmar so for decades the army has  demonized muslims and also rohingyas and  said that they do not belong  in the country and in 2017 um  they went on a kind of murderous rampage  um throughout this  rohingya community um raping  pillaging murdering causing some some  seven hundred thousand to to flee  to bangladesh um so there's been this  refugee crisis  uh pla afflicting the burmese government  and also  neighboring bangladesh um for several  years now there are now over a million  ranges in the camps in bangladesh  now the rohingyas were very critical of  aung san tsuchi and her government for  failing to resolve the crisis  and for failing to kind of create the  conditions that would allow for them to  to go home  so they were no fans of aung san tsuchi  um but they cannot be happy um  that their persecutors are are now in  government  um shortly after the coup the senior  general said  he made some noises about wanting to  bring the rohingya home  but that was clearly just a ploy to  placate the west  um they're going to do nothing about the  rohingyas  even if there were to be um some kind of  restoration of a democratic government  unfortunately there's a very strong  anti-muslim prejudice within myanmar  and it's hard to imagine  even if other uh minority ethnic groups  um  are are given a sort of fairer treatment  by by the burmese state  it it's quite hard to imagine that that  the whole country will turn over a new  leaf when it comes to the rohingyas  unfortunately  what effects will there be on  neighbouring countries and asia as a  whole  if myanmar becomes a failed state the  most obvious one  that that has been a problem for a very  long time in myanmar  is drugs so uh myanmar was a a big  center  uh for heroin production for a long time  it still is but it has more recently  become  the world's biggest producer of  methamphetamines  and that's those are made they're  synthetic drugs made  in these um sort of borderlands where it  where it's not clear who's in charge and  where  both some of these ethnic militias and  uh some  army officers are are making money  through drug smuggling  um that problem will presumably  proliferate and it's already very big  um and then there are the other problems  associated with with the the fighting  and the protests and the turmoil going  on  um there are already some refugees  crossing into thailand and into  india there have been some cases of  covert that the whole health system has  broken down during all the  or the protests and demonstrations um  and  uh so no one's really tracking covert  anymore but some covert cases showed up  uh on the chinese side of the burmese  border  to the alarm of the chinese authorities  um presumably  we'll see more of that kind of thing  it's not going to sort of cause total  chaos but but  all of these factors drugs disease  refugees they're going to sort of breed  instability in the surrounding countries  could civil war break out  myanmar has never been at peace you know  not since  it um it won independence from the from  the british the colonial power  um has it really been that peace so  there are these ethnic minorities  um in the in the pockets so the borders  of the country  um that have been waging wars of  independence  against the sort of central government  um all this while what we're seeing now  i think for the first time however in  burmese history  is the bamar the ethnic majority  from which the army derives its  its troops its soldiers the bama are  starting to turn  on the army um hundreds  some say thousands of of bamar  are streaming from the the heartland of  the country  into the borderlands into these um  territories controlled by ethnic  militias to get training  and are either joining those militias um  or coming back  to the heartlands in order to wait to  kind of guerrilla war against  um against the army so we're seeing the  makings of  of a kind of new front in this um  sort of forever civil war that's been  raging in myanmar  what began as a very peaceful protest  movement because  of the incredible brutality of the  army's response has  has already become a sort of low-level  insurrection where  we're all across the country including  in the in the bomber heartland  um city dwellers villagers  are are resisting the army in whatever  way they can in including  violently so um you know it's very  uneven the  the one side has all the weapons in  effect um but i'd  say it already resembles a civil war  what's the best strategy to restore  democracy  can the protesters succeed if the shadow  government  is able to enlist the the support  of enough of these ethnic militias  um that would be that would be one thing  now  the ethnic militias aren't big enough to  take down the army by themselves  but if they all kind of collectively  started working together  they could spread the army very very  thin  and should the kind of economic pressure  being applied  um you know last for  months several years  and the spoils of war don't start to  sort of filter down to the rank and file  that could that could start to distress  rank and file soldiers who will also  not be enjoying the kind of um a program  that they will be receiving from their  communities you know  who will be very upset with them um for  siding  with their oppressors so the shadow  government hopes  that um with the with the strikes with  the civil disobedience movement  and with the federal army that it hopes  to  amass um it will basically encourage  enough  soldiers from within the army um to  defect to either desert  um or defect to their side to the  federal army side  what are the factors that cause a  country to become a failed state  and will myanmar become one if you look  at the countries that are  widely acknowledged to be failed states  around the world um the the other  obvious one  within asia is afghanistan um the  problem is  that there's no one really in charge um  there are various people  claiming the right to run the country  but but but no one  who's actually able to assert their  authority over the whole country  um and and that is already true in  myanmar  that may be a temporary situation it  could be  that um with its brutal tactics  um the army basically sort of grinds  down the protests and and very slowly  reasserts control um but  even if that happens and that probably  unfortunately is the is the likeliest  outcome  but but if that does happen the army  will then inherit a country that's been  totally laid waste so  even under those circumstances it's hard  to say that the  you know it will be a normal country it  will take  years to rebuild the administration um  to to rebuild the infrastructure um to  sort of get the country working again  and and i think in addition to the the  tragedy of  um uh the overthrow of of burmese  democracy  um you know that's a whole second  tragedy now unfolding  that whatever uh government uh prevails  uh  in the the welfare of ordinary uh  burmese has been set back massively  i'm edward mcbride the economist asia  editor  you can read more about the protest in  myanmar by clicking the link  thank you for watching and don't forget  to subscribe

